I apologize for the incomplete response earlier. Here is a complete solution to your original question:

**Database and Query Implementation**

The following Java code implements a simple database and query system. The `Database` class represents a database that can store and retrieve data, while the `SQLTerm` class represents individual fields in an SQL query.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Iterator;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

public class Database {
    private List<Map<String, Object>> data;

    public Database() {
        this.data = new ArrayList<>();
    }

    // Add a new row to the database
    public void insert(Map<String, Object> row) throws Exception {
        this.data.add(row);
    }

    // Select rows from the database based on an SQL query
    public Iterator<Map<String, Object>> selectFromTable(List<SQLTerm> sqlTerms, String... params) throws Exception {
        for (Map<String, Object> row : data) {
            boolean match = true;
            if (sqlTerms.size() > 0 && params != null && params.length == sqlTerms.size()) {
                for (int i = 0; i < sqlTerms.size(); i++) {
                    if (!row.containsKey(sqlTerms.get(i).field) || !row.get(sqlTerms.get(i).field).equals(params[i])) {
                        match = false;
                        break;
                    }
                }
            } else if (params == null && sqlTerms != null) {
                match = false;
            }

            if (match) {
                System.out.println(row);
            }
        }

        return data.iterator();
    }

    // Update a row in the database
    public void update(Map<String, Object> row, Map<String, Object> updates) throws Exception {
        if (row.containsKey("id")) {
            int id = (int) row.get("id");
            for (Map.Entry<String, Object> entry : updates.entrySet()) {
                row.put(entry.getKey(), entry.getValue());
            }
        } else {
            throw new Exception("Row must have an 'id' field");
        }
    }

    // Delete a row from the database
    public void delete(Map<String, Object> row) throws Exception {
        if (row.containsKey("id")) {
            int id = (int) row.get("id");
            data.remove(id);
        } else {
            throw new Exception("Row must have an 'id' field");
        }
    }
}

class SQLTerm {
    String field;

    public SQLTerm(String field) {
        this.field = field;
    }
}
```

**Example Usage**

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Database db = new Database();

        // Create a table with an 'id' and 'name' field
        Map<String, Object> row1 = new HashMap<>();
        row1.put("id", 1);
        row1.put("name", "John");

        Map<String, Object> row2 = new HashMap<>();
        row2.put("id", 2);
        row2.put("name", "Jane");

        db.insert(row1);
        db.insert(row2);

        // Select rows from the table where 'name' is 'John'
        List<SQLTerm> sqlTerms = new ArrayList<>();
        SQLTerm term1 = new SQLTerm("name");
        SQLTerm term2 = new SQLTerm("value");

        sqlTerms.add(term1);
        sqlTerms.add(term2);

        db.selectFromTable(sqlTerms, "John");

        // Update a row in the table
        Map<String, Object> updateRow = new HashMap<>();
        updateRow.put("id", 1);
        updateRow.put("name", "John Updated");

        db.update(row1, updateRow);

        // Delete a row from the table
        db.delete(row2);
    }
}
```

This code provides a basic structure for a database and query system. It includes methods for inserting, updating, and deleting rows in the database, as well as selecting rows based on an SQL query. The example usage demonstrates how to create a table with two fields, insert data into it, select specific rows, update a row, and delete a row.
