**Database and Query Implementation**

The following Java code implements a simple database and query system using a HashMap to store data.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Iterator;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

public class Database {
    private List<Map<String, Object>> data;

    public Database() {
        this.data = new ArrayList<>();
    }

    // Add a new row to the database
    public void insert(Map<String, Object> row) throws Exception {
        this.data.add(row);
    }

    // Select rows from the database based on an SQL query
    public Iterator<Map<String, Object>> selectFromTable(List<SQLTerm> sqlTerms, String... params) throws Exception {
        for (Map<String, Object> row : data) {
            boolean match = true;
            if (sqlTerms.size() > 0 && params != null && params.length == sqlTerms.size()) {
                for (int i = 0; i < sqlTerms.size(); i++) {
                    String term = sqlTerms.get(i).term;
                    Object value = params[i];
                    if (!row.containsKey(term) || !row.get(term).equals(value)) {
                        match = false;
                        break;
                    }
                }
            } else if (sqlTerms.size() > 0 && params == null) {
                for (int i = 0; i < sqlTerms.size(); i++) {
                    String term = sqlTerms.get(i).term;
                    if (!row.containsKey(term)) {
                        match = false;
                        break;
                    }
                }
            }

            if (match) {
                System.out.println(row);
            }
        }

        return data.iterator();
    }

    // Update a row in the database
    public void update(Map<String, Object> id, Map<String, Object> updates) throws Exception {
        for (Map.Entry<String, Object> entry : data.iterator()) {
            if (entry.getKey().equals(id)) {
                entry.putAll(updates);
                break;
            }
        }
    }

    // Delete a row from the database
    public void delete(Map<String, Object> id) throws Exception {
        data.remove((Object) id);
    }
}

class SQLTerm {
    String term;
    int pos;

    public SQLTerm(String term, int pos) {
        this.term = term;
        this.pos = pos;
    }
}
```

**Example Usage**

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Database db = new Database();

        // Insert rows into the database
        Map<String, Object> row1 = new HashMap<>();
        row1.put("name", "John");
        row1.put("age", 30);
        db.insert(row1);

        Map<String, Object> row2 = new HashMap<>();
        row2.put("name", "Jane");
        row2.put("age", 25);
        db.insert(row2);

        // Select rows from the database where 'name' is 'John'
        List<SQLTerm> sqlTerms = new ArrayList<>();
        sqlTerms.add(new SQLTerm("name", 0));
        sqlTerms.add(new SQLTerm("value", 1));

        db.selectFromTable(sqlTerms, "John");

        // Update a row in the database
        Map<String, Object> updates = new HashMap<>();
        updates.put("age", 31);
        db.update(row1, updates);

        // Delete a row from the database
        db.delete(row2);
    }
}
```

This code provides a basic structure for a database and query system using a HashMap to store data. It includes methods for inserting, updating, and deleting rows in the database, as well as selecting rows based on an SQL query. The example usage demonstrates how to create a database, insert rows into it, select specific rows, update a row, and delete a row.
